  "rewrites": {
   "3": {
    "original": "In addition, supporters of the merger didn't address the reality that a key element in its wholesale service erosion was that the predominantly religious-affiliated Holy Redeemer paid about $50,000,000 to Abington to cover its $100,000,000-a-year debt.",
    "rewrite": "Moreover, backers of the merger did not consider the fact that one major factor in its loss of wholesale customers was Holy Redeemer paying Abington around $50,000,000 to settle a $100,000,000 annual debt."
   },
   "9": {
    "original": "For one thing, the 85-year-old lifelong Democrat loaded with pies from her own bakery and donated toys from the Stepping Stones publisher from Glenside at the end of a \"banner year\" for her political action committee.",
    "rewrite": "Firstly, the 85-year-old lifelong Democrat arrived laden with pies from her bakery and donated toys from the Stepping Stones publisher of Glenside following a \"banner year\" for her political action committee."
   },
   "5": {
    "original": "Ultimately, the merger spelled trouble for women and so the opposition merited a retreat from the plan by Abington Health and Holy Redeemer Health System officials.",
    "rewrite": "In the end, the merger led to difficulties for women, causing officials from Abington Health and Holy Redeemer Health System to reconsider the plan."
   },
   "2": {
    "original": "Whether run by a public, corporate, or church governing body, any hospital providing medical care should be geared toward saving lives, not taking them.",
    "rewrite": "No matter who runs it\u2014a public entity, a corporation, or a church\u2014any hospital that offers medical services should focus on saving lives rather than ending them."
   },
   "1": {
    "original": "And most worrisome is their potential effect of making abortion more burdensome and cost-prohibitive for women already having a hard time securing it.",
    "rewrite": "The most concerning aspect is the potential impact of increasing the burden and financial strain on women who are struggling to access abortion care."
   },
   "6": {
    "original": "On her way to Holy Redeemer Memorial Hospital on Swim Club Road, Democrat leader Karen McCormick's voice was already cracking.",
    "rewrite": "As Karen McCormick, the Democrat leader, traveled along Swim Club Road towards Holy Redeemer Memorial Hospital, her voice was starting to crack."
   },
   "8": {
    "original": "And as always, Sacks proved indomitable.",
    "rewrite": "Sacks once again demonstrated indefatigability."
   },
   "4": {
    "original": "So why not carry on in the traditional partnership if for no other reasons than to promote continued cooperation and ensure the continued financial health of both institutions?",
    "rewrite": "So why not continue in the traditional partnership to promote ongoing cooperation and ensure the sustaining financial health of both institutions for no other reason?"
   }
  },
  "final_span": "undergo expensive remodeling and adoption of hospital-like standards to qualify for licensing. The most concerning aspect is the potential impact of increasing the burden and financial strain on women who are struggling to access abortion care.\nNo matter who runs it\u2014a public entity, a corporation, or a church\u2014any hospital that offers medical services should focus on saving lives rather than ending them.\nMoreover, backers of the merger did not consider the fact that one major factor in its loss of wholesale customers was Holy Redeemer paying Abington around $50,000,000 to settle a $100,000,000 annual debt. So why not continue in the traditional partnership to promote ongoing cooperation and ensure the sustaining financial health of both institutions for no other reason?\nIn the end, the merger led to difficulties for women, causing officials from Abington Health and Holy Redeemer Health System to reconsider the plan.\nAs Karen McCormick, the Democrat leader, traveled along Swim Club Road towards Holy Redeemer Memorial Hospital, her voice was starting to crack.\n\"We're running out of heroes,\" she said bluntly of this being the late Virginia Marie Sacks's last mission to the Montgomery County prison in a North Wales sunless, cold and rainy morn.\nSacks once again demonstrated indefatigability.\nFirstly, the 85-year-old lifelong Democrat arrived laden with pies from her bakery and donated toys from the Stepping Stones publisher of Glenside following a \"banner year\" for her political action committee.\n\"For all the cynics out there, who had a Democrat on the GOP hierarchy on Limerick Avenue, I'd like to put it this way: We knocked the guy off the ballot,\" she said Wednesday while donning a Montgomery County Democratic Campaign Committee pin on a judge at the party's headquarters in Norristown.\nTrue, if that means anything. The gimpy-footed winner, John R. G",
